So `replace_layer` just does `self.layer = layer;` — it replaces the Layer struct entirely. This means any Loro observer set up on the old Layer's LoroDoc would be lost, because the old LoroDoc is dropped.

But in our ReplaceLayer handler in actor.rs, the observer is set up AFTER the replace:
```rust
if is_new {
    let mut unit = LayerUnit::new_empty();  // create empty unit
    ...
    state.units.insert(layer_name.clone(), unit);
}
// replace the layer content with snapshot
unit.replace_layer(layer);  // this replaces the inner Layer


```

The observer gets attached to the new Layer after the replacement happens, so that's fine. For the channel layer though, there's no second replace — the second message comes through `ApplyUpdateWithResult` instead, so I need to trace through how that applies updates and whether it triggers the observer.

